      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make the tomato sauce"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat {{extra-virgin-olive-oil}} of olive oil in a saucepan over medium heat. Add {{garlic}} garlic and cook for 1 minute until fragrant but not coloured. Add {{tinned-tomatoes}} tinned tomatoes, crushing them with a spoon. Season with {{salt-table}} of the salt. Simmer for 20 minutes until thick. Stir in half the {{basil}} basil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make and par-cook the gnocchi"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bake {{potatoes-floury}} potatoes at 200C for 50 to 60 minutes until completely soft. Rice while hot, cool for 5 minutes, then mix in {{00-flour}} flour, {{eggs}} egg, and remaining {{salt-table}} salt to form a soft dough. Roll into ropes and cut into 2cm pieces. Cook in batches in well-salted boiling water until they float, about 2 minutes. Lift out and place in the tomato sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Bake"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Preheat the oven to 220C. Tip the gnocchi and sauce into a large baking dish. Scatter {{mozzarella}} mozzarella over and dust with {{parmesan}} Parmesan. Bake for 15 minutes until the cheese is melted and golden in patches. Scatter the remaining {{basil}} basil over and serve from the dish."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "The gnocchi are mushy after baking",
              "cause": "They were fully cooked in the water before going into the oven.",
              "fix": "Pull the gnocchi from the water the moment they float -- they will finish cooking in the oven. Leaving them in the water longer makes them too soft to hold up to baking."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gnocchi alla sorrentina comes from Sorrento, the coastal town above the Amalfi coast. The dish is made with cow's milk mozzarella rather than buffalo mozzarella. In the traditional version, it is baked in small individual terracotta pots. It is one of the most popular dishes in Sorrento restaurants and is made in homes across Campania."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}